WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:03.100
Here's a question for you.

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:05.860
Remember when the future seemed to be a place with

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:10.420
flying cars and robots and
people traveling through space?

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.980
Back then, technology meant promise and potential.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:19.640
Americans were much more optimistic about
the role it would play in their daily lives.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.920
Fast forward to today and
that optimism seems to be vanishing.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:36.840
Reports of automation, artificial intelligence,
and self-driving vehicles

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.760
replacing human workers are everywhere.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.260
Even with near record low unemployment,

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.660
Americans are more and more concerned
about losing their jobs

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.620
because of new technologies.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:55.380
Jobs in manufacturing, in the service sector,
and in retail are especially vulnerable.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:58.260
Here's one example straight from the headlines.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.620
Nearly three million Americans drive trucks for a living.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.940
If self-driving technology develops at its current pace,

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:09.360
these jobs could vanish
in the next ten to fifteen years.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.760
In addition, accountants, doctors and lawyers

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.560
were once thought to be impervious to disruption.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.620
But even these better-paying professions

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:22.240
could be redefined with massive
shifts in how work is done.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.680
Technological disruption has come as
wages have stagnated for decades,

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:31.600
income inequality has increased,
and the middle class has shrunk.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.760
So how can the United States prepare for this disruption

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:38.280
and build new opportunities for its workers?

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:45.440
The past might offers some answers.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.080
More than a hundred years ago,

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.380
during the height of the Industrial Revolution,

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.960
new technologies gave rise to new ways of working.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:58.320
Technologies like electricity, the steam engine,
the telephone, and the automobile

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.620
all required different skills from American workers.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:06.979
The United States responded by expanding free
public education and training opportunities,

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:09.620
far outpacing any other country.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:13.760
In just thirty years, the number of 
school-age children attending high school

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.520
skyrocketed from 18 to 73 percent.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:22.240
And those completing their schooling
 went from 9 to 51 percent.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:27.260
The link between education and
good jobs is even clearer today.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:32.900
Most jobs created by retiring baby boomers
require advanced degrees or specialized training.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:38.260
Yet, more than half of all Americans have
only a high school education or less.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:42.560
That means even more good jobs
are at risk of going unfilled.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:47.000
So what can be done?

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:51.640
The first step is to see education
and work as closely linked.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:54.220
Almost half of U.S. companies complain that

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:59.300
too few graduates leave school with
the skills they need to enter the workforce.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:03.500
For younger students, this means
a greater focus on work-study programs,

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:10.120
early job-oriented counseling, internships, apprenticeships and career-related coursework.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:14.860
Companies, educational institutions and
government should help support programs

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:19.400
that allow workers to learn new skills
at every stage of their careers.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.600
So that's how workers get trained for the future,

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:27.660
but how do we ensure that those
who have acquired skills get jobs?

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:31.500
Unfortunately, the United States
has a big matching problem.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:36.060
Sometimes, this means there aren't enough
workers where jobs are being created.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:42.160
For example, the rust belt might lose manufacturing
jobs while Silicon Valley is booming.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.740
Here too we can look to the past.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:50.060
In the 1950s and 60s, Americans were
the most mobile people in the world.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:52.860
When jobs were scarce in one part of the country,

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.840
they would move to where jobs were more plentiful.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.460
That doesn't happen as much today.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:03.940
One culprit is that booming urban areas lack
affordable housing and public transportation

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.800
to get people to where the jobs are located.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.220
Another major obstacle is excessive state
and local credentialing and licensing requirements.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:15.160
For example, the country's three million teachers

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:19.480
must have state-issued licenses
in order to work in school systems.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:24.020
Yet, most states don't recognize
licenses issued by other states.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:25.920
It's not just teachers.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:31.960
Bartenders, interior decorators, 
cosmetologists, manicurists, and even florists

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:37.540
typically require some sort of state license
that's not recognized across state lines.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:41.880
Sometimes the matching problem means 
employers demand college degrees

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:47.800
for jobs that don't require them, or overlook good applicants who lack work experience.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.960
Here we can look to the future.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:54.860
Big data will empower employers to use more
precise job matching behind the scenes

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:57.380
and to share credential requirements.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:02.940
It can also help applicants showcase their
abilities to the largest number of companies.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:04.420
This all sounds pretty good,

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:09.780
but what else is needed to create more
opportunities for the American workforce?

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.300
Federal, state, and local governments should
provide better services for workers

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:18.040
transitioning from one job to another.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:21.900
Things like skills retraining and
job counseling are crucial

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:27.900
and will be even more essential for those working
in ways unimaginable to previous generations.

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:33.240
The United States currently provides just a small
fraction of what most European countries do

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:37.600
to help workers who lose their jobs
retrain for new careers.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:43.680
In addition, support systems such as health care,
sick leave, family leave, and retirement benefits,

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.940
are generally only available to full-time workers.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:50.120
Low-wage, part-time, and independent contractors,

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:53.920
are making up a larger and larger
share of the U.S. workforce.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:56.720
These employment benefits could be tied to the worker

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.040
rather than to the job throughout their careers.

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:04.260
Education, retraining, mobility, and support systems

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:06.980
might sound like tall orders for the country.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:12.660
But remember, the United States has
 overcome large challenges in the past.

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:15.240
We need to do it again.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:19.860
Nothing less than the future of
the U.S. workforce depends on it.

